---
// The research page collects preprints, theses, and other writings.
import ThemeInit from "../components/ThemeInit.astro";
import SiteNav from "../components/SiteNav.astro";
import SiteVersion from "../components/SiteVersion.astro";
import PageWatermark from "../components/PageWatermark.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <SiteVersion />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin="anonymous" />
    <link rel="preload" as="image" href="/Chow%20quotient.jpg" />
    <link
      rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,650;0,700;1,400&amp;family=STIX+Two+Text:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,700;1,400&amp;display=swap"
    />
    <ThemeInit />
    <title>Research | Suraj Dash</title>
  </head>
  <body class="research-page">
    <PageWatermark src="/Chow%20quotient.jpg" />
    <main class="page-shell">
      <SiteNav current="/research" />

    

      <section class="research-layout" aria-label="Research overview">
        <div class="publications">
          <article class="publication-card research-articles" aria-labelledby="research-articles-heading">
            <h2 id="research-articles-heading">Research articles</h2>
            <ul class="research-article-list">
              <li class="research-article">
                <div class="publication-heading">
                  <button class="research-article-toggle" id="git-title" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="git-description">
                    <span class="publication-title">Variation of GIT for Hilbert Schemes of Expansions.</span>
                  </button>
                  <span class="publication-document">(<a class="publication-document-label" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_mCMD9BelasMUGdHWn0Hpot0tFwwfWkE/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Draft available</a>)</span>
                </div>
                <button class="research-description-toggle" id="git-description-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="git-description" aria-describedby="git-title">
                  <span class="description-toggle-label">Show description</span>
                </button>
                <div class="research-description" id="git-description" role="region" aria-labelledby="git-title" aria-hidden="true" inert>
                  <div class="research-description-clip">
                    <div class="research-description-layout">
                    <div class="research-description-content illustrated-description">
                      <p>
                        Let <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="the pair X, D"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></math> be a smooth projective simple normal crossings pair. Expansions of <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="X"><mi>X</mi></math> along <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="D"><mi>D</mi></math> retain information about point configurations approaching the boundary that the ordinary Hilbert scheme <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="Hilbert scheme of n points on X"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Hilb</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></math> may forget. Logarithmic Hilbert stacks impose transversality and stability on subschemes of these expansions. Building on the GIT construction of Gulbrandsen–Halle–Hulek, we vary the torus linearisation on the full relative Hilbert scheme to study the resulting walls and birational transformations. These wall crossings also provide a way to compute invariants of logarithmic Hilbert stacks using the geometry of ordinary Hilbert schemes.
                      </p>
                      <p id="git-coarse-paragraph">
                        For a smooth pointed curve, <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="Hilbert scheme of two points on X equals the second symmetric power of X"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Hilb</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></math> records the collision of two points at <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="D"><mi>D</mi></math> as <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="2 D"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, whereas the logarithmic Hilbert stack retains their configuration on an expansion. The comparison is a <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="1, 2"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>-weighted blow-up at <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="2 D"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, as illustrated.
                      </p>
                    <aside class="diagram-panel" id="git-coarse-diagram" aria-labelledby="git-coarse-diagram-title">
                      <div class="diagram-panel-heading">
                        <h3 class="diagram-map-title" id="git-coarse-diagram-title"><math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="LogHilb squared of the projective line with infinity"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">LogHilb</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>∞</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></math><span class="diagram-map-target"><svg class="diagram-map-arrow" viewBox="0 0 32 12" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M2 6H26" stroke-linecap="round" /><path d="m23 2 5 4-5 4" /></svg><math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="maps to Hilb squared of the projective line"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Hilb</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></math></span></h3>
                      </div>
                      <a class="diagram-image-link" href="/diagrams/loghilb2-p1-coarse.svg?v=d3ddebd214c1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open the LogHilb diagram at full size in a new tab">
                        <img src="/diagrams/loghilb2-p1-coarse.svg?v=d3ddebd214c1" width="1502" height="889" alt="Coarse LogHilb²(ℙ¹, D) over the chart U, mapping by the blow-up of (x², y) to U = 𝔸² inside Hilb²(ℙ¹) = ℙ². The coarse space and the dotted neighbourhood U are filled sky blue; the exceptional curve E is red and the discriminant Δ is green." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                      </a>
                      <p class="diagram-caption">Select the diagram to view full size.</p>
                    </aside>
                      <p>
                        For general SNC pairs, we construct finite Hilbert–Mumford criteria for GIT semistability of associated zero-cycles, determined by their support types. For smooth pointed curves, we classify the GIT walls—bubbling and discriminant—and their birational transformations, including walls sensitive to infinitesimal scheme structure. We construct a discriminant-only VGIT path, followed by a semiample contraction to <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="Hilbert scheme of n points on X"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Hilb</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>, and factor its wall-crossing diagram. This yields an explicit presentation of the integral Chow ring of <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="logarithmic Hilbert stack of n points on X relative to D"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">LogHilb</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></math>.
                      </p>
                      <p id="git-toric-paragraph">
                        In genus zero, the full relative Hilbert scheme and the logarithmic Hilbert stack are toric; we determine the relative Mori and nef cones of the former over the expansion base, and the absolute Mori and nef cones of the latter.
                      </p>
                    <aside class="diagram-panel" id="git-toric-diagram" aria-labelledby="git-toric-diagram-title">
                      <div class="diagram-panel-heading">
                        <h3 id="git-toric-diagram-title">Toric <math class="inline-math" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" aria-label="LogHilb squared of the projective line with infinity"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">LogHilb</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>∞</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></math></h3>
                      </div>
                      <a class="diagram-image-link" href="/diagrams/loghilb2-p1-toric.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open the toric LogHilb diagram at full size in a new tab">
                        <img src="/diagrams/loghilb2-p1-toric.svg" width="2000" height="687" alt="Toric diagram showing successive subdivisions of the triangular sector from Σ for ℙ³ to Σ₃,₃ and then Σ for Q₀, with w₃ = 3v₀ + 2v₁ + v₂ and w₂ = 2v₀ + v₁; the polyhedral diagram on the right has labelled vertices v₀, v₁, v₂, v₃, w₃ and w₂." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                      </a>
                      <p class="diagram-caption">Select the diagram to view full size.</p>
                    </aside>
                    </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="research-article">
                <div class="publication-heading">
                  <button class="research-article-toggle" id="surfaces-title" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="surfaces-description">
                    <span class="publication-title">Wall-crossings in Hilbert scheme of expansion of surfaces</span>
                  </button>
                  <span class="publication-status">(In preparation)</span>
                </div>
                <button class="research-description-toggle" id="surfaces-description-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="surfaces-description" aria-describedby="surfaces-title">
                  <span class="description-toggle-label">Show description</span>
                </button>
                <div class="research-description" id="surfaces-description" role="region" aria-labelledby="surfaces-title" aria-hidden="true" inert>
                  <div class="research-description-clip">
                    <div class="research-description-layout">
                      <div class="research-description-content">
                        <p>We are extending the wall-crossing analysis to smooth projective surface pairs.  New wall crossings appear already in length two, including a Francia flip. For the projective plane with a line as boundary, we compute the integral Chow ring of the logarithmic Hilbert stack and the integral operational Chow ring of its coarse moduli space. Using the length-two wall crossing, we determine numerical cones, establish non-toricity for the logarithmic Hilbert stack of the projective plane with a line as boundary, and compute its motivic class using Göttsche’s results on ordinary Hilbert schemes.</p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li class="research-article">
                <div class="publication-heading">
                  <button class="research-article-toggle" id="stability-title" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="stability-description">
                    <span class="publication-title">K-stability of singular del Pezzo surfaces.</span>
                  </button>
                  <span class="publication-status">(In preparation)</span>
                </div>
                <p class="publication-collaborators">
                  With Kristin DeVleming, Fernando Figueroa, Gomathy Ganapathy, Myeong Jae Jeon, Tyson Klingner, Daniel Mallory, and Ying Wang.
                </p>
                <div class="research-description" id="stability-description" role="region" aria-labelledby="stability-title" aria-hidden="true" inert>
                  <div class="research-description-clip">
                    <!-- Project description can be added here. -->
                  </div>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </article>

         

          <article class="publication-card">
            <h2>Theses</h2>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <div class="publication-heading thesis-heading">
                  <span class="publication-title"><strong>PhD thesis:</strong> Variation of GIT for Hilbert Schemes of Expansions.</span>
                  <span class="publication-status">(Expected 2027)</span>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Master’s thesis:</strong>
                Logarithmic construction of the moduli space of admissible covers
                <span class="publication-document">(<a href="http://tesi.cab.unipd.it/65771" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Thesis</a>)</span>.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="publication-card">
            <h2>Other writings</h2>
            <p class="writing-disclaimer">
              These notes record my personal understanding of the material, based on the references cited in the bibliography. They are intended as expository notes and contain no claim of original mathematical contribution. Despite my efforts to present the material accurately, the notes may contain errors, omissions, or imprecise statements. Any such shortcomings are entirely my own.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Atiyah Flops, Francia Flips and affine VGIT (<a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/10blwS9cu33pzUixmar2m_f1ih_GBrc8Y/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Notes</a>).</li>
              <li>Logarithmic geometry (<a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1ybdkVjIpuBcMdhx00Hk2GzBNcbIhXA5d/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Notes</a>).</li>
              <li>
                A gentle introduction to the Virtual Fundamental Class (<a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1al7tgb-Kv1DnJGijHScsOXLODVi0fTl5/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Notes</a>).
                <span class="writing-context">Notes for Junior QFT seminar, UT Austin</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                Moduli of ASD connections and Donaldson invariants (<a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1VxpPCmAxMw0Mhz713AYsUZs-Dhr58TSG/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Notes</a>).
                <span class="writing-context">Moduli in geometry seminar, UT Austin</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                Fourier-Mukai transforms and the Bondal-Orlov theorem (<a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eYSlpKjiaJmqES8cNhCRekXO1P6yZVuZ/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Notes</a>).
                <span class="writing-context">Notes for Kylerec workshop, 2023</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                A gentle introduction to Bridgeland stability (<a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1nYZW--ILkuTi38VXJfMJLgMZ4XeL0vke/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Notes</a>).
                <span class="writing-context">Notes for literature seminar, UT Austin</span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <p class="background-caption">
        Watermark: Chow quotient of <span class="caption-math">LogHilb<sup>2</sup>(ℙ<sup>1</sup>, ∞)</span>.
      </p>
      <SiteFooter />
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<script>
  const researchCard = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".research-articles");
  const researchToggles = Array.from(
    researchCard?.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(".research-article-toggle") ?? []
  );
  const descriptionToggles = Array.from(
    researchCard?.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(".research-description-toggle") ?? []
  );
  let descriptionOpener: HTMLButtonElement | null = null;
  let cancelDescriptionScroll = () => {};

  // User input takes priority over an in-progress description transition.
  window.addEventListener("wheel", () => cancelDescriptionScroll(), { passive: true });
  window.addEventListener("touchstart", () => cancelDescriptionScroll(), { passive: true });
  window.addEventListener("pointerdown", () => cancelDescriptionScroll(), { passive: true });
  window.addEventListener("resize", () => cancelDescriptionScroll());
  window.addEventListener("pagehide", () => cancelDescriptionScroll());
  document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
    if (["Escape", "Tab", "ArrowUp", "ArrowDown", "PageUp", "PageDown", "Home", "End", " "].includes(event.key)) {
      cancelDescriptionScroll();
    }
  });

  function setDescriptionOpen(toggle: HTMLButtonElement, open: boolean) {
    const article = toggle.closest<HTMLElement>(".research-article");
    const description = article?.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".research-description");
    if (!article || !description) return;

    toggle.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(open));
    article.dataset.expanded = String(open);
    description.setAttribute("aria-hidden", String(!open));
    description.inert = !open;
    article.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(".research-description-toggle").forEach((cue) => {
      cue.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(open));
      const label = cue.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".description-toggle-label");
      if (label) label.textContent = open ? "Hide description" : "Show description";
    });
  }

  function toggleDescription(toggle: HTMLButtonElement, trigger: HTMLButtonElement) {
    cancelDescriptionScroll();
    const shouldOpen = toggle.getAttribute("aria-expanded") !== "true";
    const switchingDescriptions = shouldOpen && researchToggles.some((other) =>
      other !== toggle && other.getAttribute("aria-expanded") === "true"
    );
    const initialTop = toggle.getBoundingClientRect().top;
    const root = document.documentElement;
    if (switchingDescriptions) root.dataset.descriptionSwitching = "true";

    researchToggles.forEach((other) => setDescriptionOpen(other, other === toggle && shouldOpen));
    descriptionOpener = shouldOpen ? trigger : null;
    if (!switchingDescriptions) return;

    let frame = 0;
    let cancelled = false;
    cancelDescriptionScroll = () => {
      cancelled = true;
      cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
      delete root.dataset.descriptionSwitching;
      cancelDescriptionScroll = () => {};
    };

    const positionHeading = (top: number) => {
      const currentTop = toggle.getBoundingClientRect().top;
      window.scrollTo({
        top: window.scrollY + currentTop - top,
        behavior: "instant",
      });
    };
    if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches) {
      positionHeading(24);
      cancelDescriptionScroll();
      return;
    }

    const animations = Array.from(researchCard?.querySelectorAll(".research-description") ?? [])
      .flatMap((description) => description.getAnimations());
    let settled = false;
    Promise.allSettled(animations.map((animation) => animation.finished)).then(() => { settled = true; });
    const started = performance.now();
    const animate = (now: number) => {
      if (cancelled) return;
      const progress = Math.min(1, (now - started) / 320);
      const eased = progress * progress * (3 - 2 * progress);
      // Follow the selected heading during the collapse, before each frame is painted.
      positionHeading(initialTop + (24 - initialTop) * eased);
      if (progress < 1 || !settled) frame = requestAnimationFrame(animate);
      else cancelDescriptionScroll();
    };
    frame = requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  }

  researchToggles.forEach((toggle) => {
    toggle.addEventListener("click", () => toggleDescription(toggle, toggle));
  });

  descriptionToggles.forEach((cue) => {
    const toggle = researchToggles.find((other) => other.getAttribute("aria-controls") === cue.getAttribute("aria-controls"));
    if (!toggle) return;
    cue.addEventListener("click", () => toggleDescription(toggle, cue));
  });

  researchCard?.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
    if (event.key !== "Escape") return;
    cancelDescriptionScroll();
    const openToggle = researchToggles.find((toggle) => toggle.getAttribute("aria-expanded") === "true");
    if (!openToggle) return;
    const focusTarget = descriptionOpener ?? openToggle;
    setDescriptionOpen(openToggle, false);
    descriptionOpener = null;
    focusTarget.focus({ preventScroll: true });
    event.preventDefault();
  });
</script>

<style>
  :global(:root) {
    --bg: #dfeaf0;
    --surface: #fffaf2;
    --text: #24313a;
    --heading: #304957;
    --muted: #4d606c;
    --accent: #98403a;
    --link: #286784;
    --line: rgba(43, 68, 82, 0.22);
    --card-edge: rgba(49, 98, 118, 0.42);
    --body-glow: rgba(58, 103, 127, 0.1);
    --shadow: rgba(36, 49, 58, 0.09);
    --background-overlay-start: rgba(223, 234, 240, 0.50);
    --background-overlay-end: rgba(223, 234, 240, 0.60);
    --card-bg: rgba(252, 250, 246, 0.90);
    --caption: #405661;
    --caption-shadow: rgba(252, 250, 246, 0.85);
  }

  :global(*) { box-sizing: border-box; }
  :global(body) {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    margin: 0;
    min-height: 100vh;
    font-family: "DM Sans", system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
    font-size: 19.8px;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text);
    background: radial-gradient(circle at top right, var(--body-glow), transparent 34rem), var(--bg);
    transition: color 180ms ease, background-color 180ms ease;
  }
  :global(.page-watermark) { z-index: -1; }
  :global(p) { color: var(--text); line-height: 1.6; }
  :global(a) { color: var(--link); text-decoration-thickness: .08em; text-underline-offset: .16em; }
  .research-layout :global(a) { font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; }
  .research-layout :global(a:hover), .research-layout :global(a:focus-visible) { text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: .1em; text-underline-offset: .18em; }
  :global(strong) { font-weight: 650; }
  h2 { font-family: "DM Sans", system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif; font-weight: 600; color: var(--heading); }
  .page-shell { position: relative; z-index: 1; width: min(1100px, calc(100% - 48px)); min-height: 100vh; margin: 0 auto; padding: 32px 0 112px; }

  .research-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 32px;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .publications { display: grid; gap: 18px; width: min(100%, 980px); margin: 0 auto; }

  .publication-card {
    position: relative;
    padding: 22px 24px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 20px;
    background: var(--card-bg);
    box-shadow: 0 14px 36px var(--shadow);
  }

  .publication-card::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    top: 18px;
    bottom: 18px;
    left: 0;
    width: 3px;
    border-radius: 0 999px 999px 0;
    background: var(--card-edge);
    box-shadow: 1px 0 8px var(--card-edge);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .publication-card h2 {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-size: 1.32rem;
    color: var(--heading);
  }

  .publication-card ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    color: var(--text);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .publication-card li { padding-left: 4px; }

  .publication-heading {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.25rem 0.45rem;
  }

  .thesis-heading { display: block; }

  .publication-title { min-width: 0; }

  .publication-status,
  .publication-document {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    font-family: "STIX Two Text", Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    font-size: 18.8px;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.4;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .publication-status { color: #925c45; }
  .publication-document { color: var(--text); }
  .publication-document-label { color: var(--link); font-weight: 600; }

  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .publication-status {
    color: #d6a387;
  }


  .research-articles {
    --description-accent: #925c45;
    --description-bg: rgba(146, 92, 69, 0.055);
  }

  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .research-articles {
    --description-accent: #d6a387;
    --description-bg: rgba(214, 163, 135, 0.065);
  }

  .research-article-list { list-style: disc; }

  .research-article-toggle {
    position: relative;
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: transparent;
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
    text-align: left;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .research-article-toggle:hover { color: var(--link); }
  .research-article-toggle:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--link); outline-offset: 5px; }

  .research-description-toggle {
    display: flex;
    width: fit-content;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.35em;
    min-height: 24px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 2px 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--link);
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: 15.18px;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-style: normal;
    line-height: 1.45;
